CAT = "ea2Income"; SEG = "INC"
# Business income, expenses, deductions and credits. Tax year 2025.
assert 240 * 0.5 == 120          # business meal
assert 6 == 18 - 12              # 2025 months of rent July to December deductible
Q = [
("In 2025 an accrual business with no applicable financial statement receives payment for services it will perform in 2025 and 2026. It may:",
 "Defer the 2026 part one year", ["Defer all of it to 2026", "Defer it over the service term", "Report none until it performs"],
 "Section 451(c) allows a one-year deferral of the part of an advance payment not earned by the end of the year of receipt. The deferred part must be included in the next year, however long the service runs."),
("Which cost does a retailer include in cost of goods sold?",
 "Freight-in on merchandise", ["Freight-out to customers", "Advertising for the goods", "Commissions to sales staff"],
 "Cost of goods sold includes the purchase price and the cost of getting goods to the business, such as freight-in. Delivery to customers, advertising and sales commissions are selling expenses."),
("For 2025, a reseller is exempt from the uniform capitalization rules if it:",
 "Meets the gross receipts test", ["Has no employees on payroll", "Uses the accrual method", "Sells only through a website"],
 "Section 263A(i) exempts a small business taxpayer, one meeting the section 448(c) gross receipts test, from UNICAP. Method of accounting, staff size and sales channel do not matter."),
("A business that first adopts the LIFO inventory method files:",
 "Form 970", ["Form 3115", "Form 8832", "Form 4562"],
 "Form 970 is the application to use LIFO, attached to the return for the first year it is used. Form 3115 is for most other method changes, including changing out of LIFO."),
("For the at-risk rules, which amount counts as at risk in a business activity?",
 "Recourse loans you must repay", ["Nonrecourse loans, as a rule", "Amounts covered by stop-loss deals", "Loans from someone with an interest"],
 "Section 465 treats as at risk cash and property contributed and amounts borrowed for which the taxpayer is personally liable. Nonrecourse debt, protected amounts and loans from interested persons generally are not."),
("A solvent sole proprietor, not in bankruptcy, has a vendor forgive $8,000 she owed for repair services she had deducted. The $8,000 is:",
 "Ordinary income to the owner", ["A purchase price reduction", "Capital gain to the owner", "Excluded if under $10,000"],
 "Canceled business debt is income under section 61(a)(11) unless an exclusion applies. A solvent taxpayer outside bankruptcy whose debt is not qualified real property or farm debt has no exclusion, and the purchase price reduction rule covers only debt for property bought, not services."),
("A sole proprietor gives $1,000 of business funds to a public charity. The gift is deducted:",
 "On Schedule A if itemizing", ["On Schedule C as an expense", "On Schedule SE as an offset", "On Form 8283 by itself"],
 "A sole proprietorship's charitable gift is a personal deduction of the owner, taken on Schedule A when itemizing. It is not a Schedule C business expense and does not reduce self-employment income."),
("An accrual corporation accrues a 2025 bonus for an employee who owns no stock. The bonus is deductible for 2025 if paid by:",
 "March 15, 2026", ["December 31, 2025", "April 15, 2026", "January 31, 2026"],
 "Compensation paid within 2 1/2 months after year end is not treated as deferred compensation, so an accrual business deducts it in the year accrued if paid within 2 1/2 months (by March 15). Later payment moves the deduction to the year paid."),
("An accrual corporation accrues salary to its 70% cash-basis shareholder in December 2025 and pays it in February 2026. The corporation deducts it:",
 "In 2026, when it is paid", ["In 2025, when accrued", "In 2025 if paid within 2 1/2 months", "Over the two years equally"],
 "Section 267(a)(2) defers an accrual payer's deduction to a related cash-method payee until the payee includes the amount in income. A more-than-50% owner is related, so the 2 1/2 month rule does not help."),
("Which benefit can an employer exclude from wages as a de minimis fringe?",
 "Occasional snacks in the break room", ["Gift cards of a small value", "Season tickets to home games", "Monthly gym membership dues"],
 "A de minimis fringe is property or a service so small and infrequent that accounting for it is unreasonable. Cash and cash equivalents such as gift cards are never de minimis, whatever the amount."),
("Ben, 16, works for his father's sole proprietorship. His wages are:",
 "Exempt from FICA tax", ["Subject to FICA tax", "Exempt from income tax", "Subject to FUTA tax"],
 "Section 3121(b)(3) excludes a child under 18 employed by a parent's sole proprietorship from FICA, and section 3306(c)(5) excludes a child under 21 from FUTA. The wages are still subject to income tax."),
("Kim works as an employee of her husband's sole proprietorship. Her wages are subject to:",
 "FICA but not FUTA", ["Neither FICA nor FUTA", "FUTA but not FICA", "Both FICA and FUTA"],
 "A spouse employed by the other spouse's business is covered for Social Security and Medicare, but section 3306(c)(5) excludes the spouse's services from federal unemployment tax."),
("A statutory employee, with box 13 checked on Form W-2, reports the wages and expenses on:",
 "Schedule C, without SE tax", ["Schedule C, with SE tax", "Form 2106 as an employee", "Schedule SE only"],
 "Statutory employees deduct business expenses on Schedule C, but the employer already withheld Social Security and Medicare, so no self-employment tax is figured on that income."),
("Lena rents a building she owns to a business in which she materially participates. Her net rental income is:",
 "Nonpassive income", ["Passive income", "Portfolio income", "Exempt income"],
 "Under the self-rental rule in Reg. 1.469-2(f)(6), net rental income from property rented to a business in which the owner materially participates is recharacterized as nonpassive, so it cannot absorb passive losses."),
("A cash-method business prepays 18 months of office rent on July 1, 2025. Its 2025 deduction covers:",
 "6 months of rent", ["All 18 months of rent", "12 months of rent", "None of the rent"],
 "The 12-month rule allows a current deduction only if the benefit ends by the earlier of 12 months after it begins or the end of the next year. An 18-month benefit fails, so rent is deducted as it applies: July to December 2025."),
("Unlike a nonbusiness bad debt, a business bad debt is deductible:",
 "When partly worthless", ["As a short-term capital loss", "Only when totally worthless", "As a long-term capital loss"],
 "Section 166(a)(2) allows a business bad debt deduction for the part charged off when the debt becomes partly worthless, as an ordinary loss. A nonbusiness bad debt must be totally worthless and is a short-term capital loss."),
("A cash-method consultant cannot collect a $4,000 fee she billed in 2025. Her bad debt deduction is:",
 "$0", ["$4,000", "$2,000", "$3,000"],
 "A cash-method taxpayer never included the fee in income, so it has no basis in the receivable and nothing to deduct. Only amounts previously included in income can become a deductible bad debt."),
("A self-employed taxpayer pays $240 for a business meal with a client in 2025. The deductible amount is:",
 "$120", ["$240", "$0", "$192"],
 "Section 274(n) limits business meals to 50% of cost, here $120. The 80% rate applies only to meals of workers under Department of Transportation hours-of-service limits."),
("A business buys baseball tickets, with no food, for a client in 2025. The cost is:",
 "Not deductible at all", ["50% deductible", "Fully deductible", "Deductible up to $25"],
 "Since 2018 section 274(a) disallows entertainment, amusement or recreation expenses even when business is discussed. Food bought separately may still qualify for the 50% meal deduction."),
("A business gives a client a $60 gift basket in 2025, its only gift to her this year. The deduction is:",
 "$25", ["$60", "$30", "$0"],
 "Section 274(b) limits the deduction for business gifts to $25 per recipient per year. Incidental costs such as engraving or shipping do not count toward the $25."),
("To use the standard mileage rate for a car the business owns, the taxpayer must:",
 "Choose it in the car's first year", ["Choose it in any later year", "Own five or more cars at once", "Claim MACRS in the first year"],
 "The standard mileage rate for an owned car must be chosen in the first year the car is used for business. It cannot be used after MACRS was claimed or for five or more cars used at the same time."),
("For tax purposes, interest on a loan is classified as business, investment or personal by:",
 "How the loan proceeds are used", ["What property secures the loan", "Who the lender of the money is", "How long the loan's term runs"],
 "Under the interest tracing rules of Temp. Reg. 1.163-8T, interest is allocated by the use of the borrowed money, not by the collateral. A home equity loan spent on equipment yields business interest."),
("A business is exempt from the section 163(j) business interest limit for 2025 if it:",
 "Meets the gross receipts test", ["Has no foreign owners at all", "Uses the cash method for all items", "Pays interest rates under 30%"],
 "Section 163(j)(3) exempts a small business that meets the section 448(c) gross receipts test, other than a tax shelter. For others, business interest is limited to 30% of adjusted taxable income plus interest income."),
("A self-employed person may take the self-employed health insurance deduction for any month in which she:",
 "Is not eligible for an employer plan", ["Has a net loss from her business", "Itemizes her deductions that year", "Has no qualifying dependents"],
 "Section 162(l) denies the deduction for months the person is eligible for a subsidized plan through an employer or a spouse's employer, and limits it to earned income from the business."),
("A sole proprietor buys overhead insurance that pays the business's rent and utilities if she is disabled for a long period. The premiums are:",
 "Deductible business expenses", ["Nondeductible personal costs", "Added to her assets' basis", "Deductible only by a C corporation"],
 "Publication 334 lists overhead insurance covering business overhead during long disability as a deductible insurance expense. Premiums for disability insurance replacing her own lost income are personal."),
("An employer's FUTA tax paid for its employees is:",
 "Deductible as a business expense", ["Credited against income tax", "Withheld from employees' pay", "Nondeductible like income tax"],
 "The employer's share of FICA and its FUTA tax are deductible taxes of the business. FUTA is paid only by the employer, never withheld from wages."),
("A business deducted $2,000 of a state tax in 2024 and receives a refund of it in 2025. The refund is:",
 "Income in 2025", ["A reduction of the 2024 deduction", "Excluded as a tax refund", "Income only if over $600"],
 "Under the tax benefit rule of section 111, a recovery of an amount deducted in an earlier year is income in the year recovered, to the extent the deduction reduced tax. The earlier return is not amended."),
("Which business is a specified service trade or business for the qualified business income deduction?",
 "A consulting practice", ["An engineering firm", "An architecture firm", "A restaurant"],
 "Section 199A(d)(2) uses the section 1202(e)(3)(A) list but drops engineering and architecture, so those are not SSTBs. Consulting remains on the list, which limits the deduction above the income threshold."),
("Above the threshold, a non-SSTB's QBI deduction is limited to the greater of 50% of W-2 wages or:",
 "25% of wages plus 2.5% of UBIA", ["20% of wages plus 5% of UBIA", "30% of wages plus 3% of UBIA", "15% of wages plus 10% of UBIA"],
 "Section 199A(b)(2)(B) caps the deduction for each business at the greater of 50% of W-2 wages or 25% of W-2 wages plus 2.5% of the unadjusted basis immediately after acquisition of qualified property."),
("Besides businesses with gross receipts of $1 million or less, the disabled access credit is open to businesses that last year had:",
 "30 or fewer full-time employees", ["25 or fewer full-time employees", "50 or fewer full-time employees", "100 or fewer full-time employees"],
 "Section 44(b) defines an eligible small business by gross receipts of $1 million or less or no more than 30 full-time employees in the prior year, counting those working 30 hours or more a week for 20 weeks."),
("To claim the small business health care tax credit, an employer must have:",
 "Fewer than 25 full-time equivalents", ["Fewer than 50 full-time equivalents", "30 or fewer full-time equivalents", "Under 100 workers of any kind"],
 "Section 45R limits the credit to employers with fewer than 25 full-time equivalent employees and average wages under an indexed limit, paying at least half of premiums for coverage bought through a SHOP Marketplace."),
("The farming loss portion of a 2025 net operating loss may be:",
 "Carried back 2 years", ["Carried back 5 years", "Carried back 3 years", "Only carried forward"],
 "Section 172(b)(1)(B) keeps a 2-year carryback for farming losses, which the taxpayer may waive. Other losses arising after 2020 are carried forward only."),
("A noncorporate taxpayer's excess business loss disallowed for 2025 is:",
 "Carried forward as an NOL", ["Lost permanently that year", "Carried back 2 tax years", "Deducted ratably over 5 years"],
 "Section 461(l)(2) treats an excess business loss disallowed in a year as part of the net operating loss carried to later years, where it is subject to the NOL rules."),
("To deduct a home office as a principal place of business, the space must be used:",
 "Regularly and exclusively", ["Regularly or exclusively", "Exclusively, at least weekly", "Regularly, mostly for work"],
 "Section 280A(c)(1) requires regular and exclusive use for the business, apart from the day care and inventory storage exceptions. Occasional use or mixed personal use fails."),
("Under the simplified method for a home office, the deduction is:",
 "$5 a square foot, up to 300 sq ft", ["$4 a square foot, up to 250 sq ft", "$6 a square foot, up to 200 sq ft", "$3 a square foot, up to 400 sq ft"],
 "Rev. Proc. 2013-13 sets a prescribed rate of $5 per square foot for up to 300 square feet, so at most $1,500, still limited to the business's gross income from the use. No home depreciation is taken that year."),
("Business equipment with an adjusted basis of $9,000 and a value of $6,000 is totally destroyed by fire with no insurance. The deductible loss is:",
 "$9,000", ["$6,000", "$3,000", "$8,900"],
 "For business property completely destroyed, the loss is the adjusted basis, less any insurance, under Reg. 1.165-7(b). The $100 reduction and 10% of AGI rules apply only to personal-use property."),
]
